WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.660
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Karolina.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.547
Welcome to "Serverless
Expeditions."

00:00:03.547 --> 00:00:05.130
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ:
Thank you, Martin.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.039
MARTIN OMANDER: You are a
Cloud Run product manager,

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.480
and I hear you've
worked in a way

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.940
to make Cloud Run
even more useful?

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.380
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ:
That's right.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.110
We've released Cloud Run
Jobs, which makes it easier

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.140
to use Cloud Run for
unattended batch jobs,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.640
like database migrations,
report generation, and so on.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.778
MARTIN OMANDER: Can't wait
to hear more about it.

00:00:25.778 --> 00:00:29.194
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.828 --> 00:00:37.370
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Developers
like Cloud Run because it lets

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.650
you run containers
in a serverless way.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.300
Containers are good
because they make

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.150
it easy to package up your
application and the libraries

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:44.815
it needs.

00:00:44.815 --> 00:00:46.190
MARTIN OMANDER:
And serverless is

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.700
good because it means your cloud
provider worries about scaling

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.737
up and down, so you
don't have to, right?

00:00:51.737 --> 00:00:52.820
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Right.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.880
So far, you've been able to
trigger Cloud Run executions

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.150
on HTTP calls or
events, like a file

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.925
being added to Cloud Storage.

00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:02.300
But there are some
workloads that

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.855
aren't well-suited for that.

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.480
MARTIN OMANDER: What
kind of workloads?

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.022
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Well, you
might have a database migration

00:01:08.022 --> 00:01:11.240
or cleanup work, an
export or import process,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.670
reports to generate, and so on.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.850
These jobs need to run
unattended without an HTTP

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.380
request from a user.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.540
Also, they should
run to completion.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.770
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, right.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.110
Back in my sysadmin
days, I had many things

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.320
that weren't web servers,
like maintenance scripts.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.550
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Exactly.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.260
These jobs often
take longer than it

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.650
takes to process an HTTP
request or an event, typically

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.373
minutes instead of seconds.

00:01:35.373 --> 00:01:36.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.600
I remember setting
up a virtual machine

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.002
to run some of those scripts.

00:01:40.002 --> 00:01:41.960
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Many
organizations do that,

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.500
but it's inefficient.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.000
You have to manage and
configure the virtual machine,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.880
and you have to remember to take
it down once your job finishes

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.130
or pay for it 24/7.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.215
Also, it can't easily scale
up to run really large jobs.

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:58.340
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, I
only used a virtual machine

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.520
for scripts like that because
there was no serverless way

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.630
of doing it back then.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.370
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ:
Well, now there is.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.350
It's called Cloud Run Jobs.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:07.910
It's still serverless.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.919
It's still running containers.

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:12.260
But it works better for
unattended workloads that

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.485
aren't triggered by HTTP calls.

00:02:14.485 --> 00:02:15.860
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds good.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.770
But I suspect it's still not
completely clear to everyone

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:19.920
how it works.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.200
We are developers, and we
like to see code and a demo.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.940
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Well, let's
hope the demo gods are with me.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.960
Imagine a company receives
invoices from its vendors

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.910
as PDF files.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.200
Here are a few examples of
what they might look like.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.690
Every night, they want to
extract the vendor names

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.380
and totals from those PDFs so
their finance team can review

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.792
and approve them the next day.

00:02:42.792 --> 00:02:43.750
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.637
I've built nightly
jobs like that before.

00:02:45.637 --> 00:02:46.720
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Right.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.430
Now here's the code.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.770
This part reads
the invoice files

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.300
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.260
This part calls the Document
AI API to extract vendor names

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.550
and totals from each invoice.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:04.590
This part writes the
invoice data to Firestore.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.650
And, finally, this part
moves the processed invoices

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.200
to another folder so
they won't get picked up

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.180
next time the job runs.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.040
MARTIN OMANDER: And,
once the job runs,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.533
how will we see the
parsed invoice data?

00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:19.200
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: We also have
this simple web page that reads

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.350
the parsed invoice data from
Firestore and displays it

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.640
to the user.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.810
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.970
Would we deploy the
invoice parsing job

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.685
like a regular
Cloud Run service?

00:03:28.685 --> 00:03:30.060
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ:
It's similar.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.300
You first build your container
with Google Cloud Build

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.440
and then create your job
with gcloud run jobs create.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.030
Let me create it right now.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.290
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.660
This code you showed me does
not listen to HTTP calls.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.307
So how do we get it to run?

00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:49.640
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Well, you
can run it from the Google

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.070
Cloud Console or
the command line.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.950
It's similar to running a
shell script on your computer,

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.320
but it runs in the cloud
and has access to all

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.430
your Google Cloud resources.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.580
Let me do that now.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:02.740
There it goes.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:03.620
MARTIN OMANDER: Mhm.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.660
Looks like something ran.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.590
Can we see the
output of the job?

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.180
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ:
Let's have a look.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.638
I'll go to the
simple web page that

00:04:10.638 --> 00:04:14.210
displays the parsed
details from the invoices.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.339
Looks like it captured
the invoice data.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:17.600
MARTIN OMANDER: Nice.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.290
But, Karolina, what if I want
to run a nightly job when I'm

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.107
not at my keyboard?

00:04:22.107 --> 00:04:24.440
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Anything
that can call the Cloud Run

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.670
admin API can start the job,
including Cloud Scheduler.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.900
Let's use Cloud Scheduler to
make our job run every night.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.190
I go to Cloud Scheduler.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.110
Create a new job.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.990
Give it a name and
a region to run in.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.590
Set a schedule for it to
run using crontab notation

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.650
and a time zone.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:47.600
Click Continue.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.000
Target type, HTTP.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.600
Enter the Cloud Run admin
API endpoint to run your job.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.060
You can find that in
the documentation.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.482
And keep HTTP method as POST.

00:04:59.482 --> 00:05:00.940
Now we have to give
Cloud Scheduler

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:02.710
permissions to start the job.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.290
I click here to
pick OAuth token.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.660
And then I select which
Service account Scheduler

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:10.010
should use to start the job.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:13.010
I know this one has
sufficient permission.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.790
You need a service account
that has the Cloud Run invoker

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.100
permission on the job.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.840
Then I click Create.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.460
Now the job will be run nightly.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.820
You won't have to be at your
keyboard at midnight, Martin.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.440
MARTIN OMANDER: That's a relief.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.470
The code you showed me
processed one invoice at a time

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:31.940
in series.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.420
If we have a thousand invoices,
that would take a long time.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:36.620
Can we speed it up somehow?

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.620
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Yes.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.940
You can tell Could Run Jobs to
start several parallel tasks.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.370
Let's say you have
a thousand invoices

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:46.070
and you start 10 parallel tasks.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.620
Your code can detect
what number task it is--

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.820
for example, 4 out of 10.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:55.240
Then your code can process
its part of the total work.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.500
For task number 4, that
would be invoices 301 to 400.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.110
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds very
useful for a large jobs,

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.600
like big old data
migration jobs that need

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.360
to process millions of records.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.607
Do you have a code
example for that?

00:06:09.607 --> 00:06:12.190
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: Let's save
that for another video, Martin.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.770
Also, it's all in
the documentation.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:16.387
The link is in the description.

00:06:16.387 --> 00:06:17.470
MARTIN OMANDER: Very good.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.930
Thanks for showing us, Karolina.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.010
And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.890
If you have questions
for Karolina or me,

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.482
let us know in the comments.

00:06:25.482 --> 00:06:27.940
Also let us know if there are
other serverless topics you'd

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.230
like to hear about.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.880
Until next time.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:35.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

